.lua
book.chapter(3, "Source text, tokens, and layout")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Names are the first interface"
.pp
A lexer turns bytes into tokens before a parser can recognize expressions.
Identifiers conventionally start lowercase for values and uppercase for
constructors, types, and modules. The implementation also accepts underscore
starts and apostrophes within identifiers. Digits may follow the first
character. Use names to expose roles: parse_count suggests a function, Count
suggests a constructor or module, and count suggests a value. Keywords such as
module, let, fn, and match are reserved syntactic markers.
.pp
Case is not merely decorative when dot notation appears. List.map is a module
path, while person.name projects a field from a record-valued expression.
Both are readable because the surrounding conventions help identify what is
being selected. A source formatter must preserve the spelling and case of
identifiers, even when it can safely adjust whitespace elsewhere. Renaming is
a binding-aware transformation, not a case-normalization pass.
.sh 2 "Numeric tokens and their boundaries"
.pp
Integer literals support decimal, hexadecimal, and binary forms. Separators
improve readability but must occur between digits; a trailing underscore or
repeated underscore is not discarded silently. Floating literals include a
fractional part or exponent. The lexer validates literal syntax before
arithmetic begins, so an invalid token should be diagnosed as a source problem,
not interpreted as a surprising runtime number.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[decimal := 1_024
hexadecimal := 0x400
binary := 0b10000000000
scientific := 1.25e2
Test.equal "integer spellings" (decimal, hexadecimal, binary) (1024, 1024, 1024)
Test.equal "exponent" scientific 125.0]=], "Several spellings, equal numeric values")
book.code("metaml-error:M0105", [=[broken := 1__0]=], "Malformed numeric separators are rejected")
.endlua
.pp
Unary negation has an unambiguous spelling, ~. Negative numeric tokens are also
recognized in suitable lexical positions, but an argument such as f -1 can be
hard to distinguish visually from subtraction. Write f (~1) in examples and
interfaces. That convention makes the syntactic boundary explicit and works
equally well when the negative argument is computed rather than literal.
Arithmetic representation and overflow are separate questions, covered next.
.sh 2 "Strings and comments"
.pp
Double-quoted strings recognize newline, tab, carriage return, quote,
backslash, zero-byte, and Unicode scalar escapes. A Unicode escape names a
scalar and encodes it as UTF-8 bytes. Invalid scalars, including surrogates, are
rejected. A raw string starts with r followed by a double quote and leaves
backslashes literal. Raw syntax is helpful for patterns and paths, but it is
not a substitute for understanding the receiving library's escaping rules.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[ordinary := "a\nb"
raw := r"a\nb"
Test.equal "escaped newline" (String.length ordinary) 3
Test.equal "raw backslash" (String.length raw) 4
Test.equal "scalar becomes UTF-8" (String.length "\u{e9}") 2
Test.equal "embedded zero" (String.length "a\0b") 3]=], "Literal syntax and byte content")
.endlua
.pp
Line comments start with two hyphens. Block comments use opening brace-hyphen
and closing hyphen-brace, and they nest. A lexer therefore needs a nesting
counter rather than a search for the first closing pair. A quote inside a
comment does not begin a language string, and a comment marker inside a string
does not begin a comment. These distinctions matter when writing a highlighter
or stripping comments: careless regular-expression substitution can change a
program's meaning before the parser ever sees it.
.lua
book.algorithm("Scan a nested block comment", {
    "Set depth to one after consuming the opening pair.",
    "While depth is positive:",
    {1, "If the next pair opens a comment, increment depth."},
    {1, "Else if it closes a comment, decrement depth."},
    {1, "Else consume the next source byte."},
    {1, "At end of input, report incomplete input or a lexical error."},
    "Resume tokenization only after depth reaches zero."
})
.endlua
.sh 2 "Hashes and dollars have distinct jobs"
.pp
A numbered dollar reference addresses a formal parameter of an enclosing tacit
definition. A numbered hash is a section hole. A named hash at the start of a
form can introduce a directive, and some other hash-prefixed text can be treated
as a comment. Prefer the unambiguous double-hyphen comment convention. Otherwise
a sentence beginning with a directive-like word can accidentally ask the REPL
to do something rather than merely annotate source.
.pp
Numbered placeholders are one-based and bounded to 1024. The lexical bound
prevents numeric conversion overflow and enormous generated parameter lists;
zero is rejected as an invalid parameter index in the appropriate later stage.
The bound is an implementation contract, not an invitation to write functions
with hundreds of numbered arguments. Readability normally deteriorates long
before that limit becomes relevant.
.sh 2 "Newlines are not freely movable"
.pp
Blocks have explicit markers such as struct/end and let/in, but the current
parser also uses newlines and source columns to recognize top-level boundaries.
Place separate definitions at column one. Indent a continued pipeline or
arithmetic expression, or surround it with parentheses. This is a practical
layout convention rather than a full offside-rule language. It also means that
older claims of completely insignificant indentation are too strong for the
implementation readers run today.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[result :=
  [1, 2, 3]
  |> List.map (fn x -> x + 1)
  |> List.sum
Test.equal "continued pipeline" result 9]=], "Make continuation visible")
.endlua
.pp
For language tools, preserve source spans even when discarding comments and
whitespace. The lexer reports tokens, diagnostics, and whether input is
incomplete. An interactive client can wait for more input after an unterminated
string; a batch client can report the same condition as an unfinished file.
Conflating incomplete input with valid empty input produces confusing REPL
behavior and makes editors lose the location of the actual problem.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Inspect lex, LexResult, and the token definitions in MetaML.hpp and MetaML.d.
The book's syntax-highlight rofflet is intentionally only a presentation lexer;
it is not used to accept programs. Try nested comments containing quote marks,
strings containing comment delimiters, and a multiline pipeline. Predict which
characters become tokens before running the examples. Then use #ast to see how
the parser groups those tokens, keeping lexical recognition distinct from
expression precedence and binding scope.
